AB  - Wheat (Triticum aestivum), a staple crop feeding billions, faces escalating threats from fungal foliar and non-foliar diseases that jeopardize global food security. Traditional visual scouting is subjective, labor intensive, and poorly suited to early or sub-symptomatic diagnosis. Artificial intelligence (AI) has laid the foundation for precision disease management by enabling quick, non-destructive, and scalable assessment of plant health in both field and breeding settings. This review synthesizes recent advances in AI-based wheat disease detection, with emphasis on deep learning and multi-modal pipelines that integrate spectral, spatial, and contextual inputs. It traces the evolution from rule-based expert systems to convolutional and attention-based architectures applied to RGB, multispectral, and hyperspectral imagery. Particular focus is placed on non-foliar and cryptic diseases, such as Fusarium head blight, take-all, and vascular wilt complexes, where latent symptom development, environmental confounders, and weak foliar signals challenge conventional image-based methods. The review critically examines key limitations, including overfitting on small or skewed datasets, spectral–spatial trade-offs, model interpretability, and the lab-to-field performance gap. In response, it outlines the potential of hybrid AI systems that couple deep learning with environment- and phenology-aware expert engines, and highlights priorities for open multimodal datasets, interpretable and edge-efficient models, and interdisciplinary collaboration to enable early, robust, and equitable disease surveillance for smallholder farming systems under a changing climate. © Akadémiai Kiadó Zrt. 2026.
