AB  - Explainable artificial intelligence (XAI) is increasingly integrated into plant disease detection to improve transparency and trust, yet the literature remains fragmented regarding which approaches are used, what datasets underpin them, and how explanations are evaluated. This PRISMA-ScR-guided scoping review maps XAI research in plant disease detection and identifies gaps limiting trustworthy, field-ready deployment. We searched six databases and arXiv, supplemented by Google Scholar and citation chasing, for studies published January 2020 to October 2025, yielding 88 eligible articles. Model architectures, XAI techniques, dataset characteristics, and evaluation strategies were extracted and synthesized using a two-axis taxonomy based on explainability scope (local vs. global) and model transparency (intrinsic vs. post-hoc). Convolutional neural networks remain dominant, with growing adoption of Vision Transformers and hybrid architectures. Post-hoc methods overwhelmingly dominate. Grad-CAM and variants appeared in 54.5 % of studies, followed by LIME (37.5 %) and SHAP (18.2 %), while intrinsic and concept-based approaches remain underexplored. The evidence base is skewed toward controlled datasets, with PlantVillage or equivalent sources underpinning 34.1 % of studies and only 20.5 % incorporating field or hybrid data. Evaluation is largely performance-centric (accuracy: 53.4 %), with rigorous explainability assessment uncommon only 2.3 % reported quantitative explainability metrics and 3.4 % incorporated expert validation. Key challenges include dataset bias, poor field generalization, qualitative treatment of explanations, and limited reproducibility. Advancing trustworthy agricultural XAI requires field-realistic benchmarks, standardized explainability metrics, and human-centered validation protocols, forming an actionable research agenda for reliable AI-driven decision-making in real farming environments. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.
