AB  - This research explores the critical intersection of deep learning and edge computing for agricultural applications. Deep learning methodologies advance how we identify and locate key elements within agricultural landscapes, from livestock to crops, weeds, and diseases. For autonomous agricultural systems operating in remote areas with limited connectivity, edge devices performing real-time detections are essential for functions such as phenotyping, weed and disease identification and mitigation, nutrient deficiency estimation, precise nutrient application, yield prediction, and crop harvesting. However, when complex detection models trained on high-performance systems are deployed to edge devices with limited computational capacities, metrics like mean average precision (mAP) and inference time often suffer. While researchers have used TensorRT optimization to enhance detection performance on edge devices, a knowledge gap exists regarding how this optimization affects metrics beyond inference speed, particularly mAP and energy consumption. This study systematically evaluates TensorRT's impact on the mAP performance of YOLOv7 and YOLOv7-tiny across two NVIDIA edge platforms: Jetson Xavier and Jetson Nano. The experiments involved fine-tuning and optimizing both models on a custom soybean plant dataset captured via unmanned aerial vehicles (UAVs) between 2020-2022, divided into 2,000 images for fine-tuning, 600 for validation, and two 600-image test batches for mixed-model statistical analysis. By examining multiple optimization options (default, FP16, and INT8), edge device types (Jetson Nano and Jetson Xavier), and input image resolutions (640 × 640, 320 × 320, and 256 × 256), the research reveals that FP16 optimization significantly improves inference speed without compromising mAP, while INT8 optimization negatively impacts recall and overall mAP for both detection models. Surprisingly, despite Jetson Xavier's superior computational specifications, Jetson Nano demonstrated better mAP performance with TensorRT optimization. Additionally, YOLOv7-tiny exhibited a better response to optimization compared to the standard YOLOv7 model. These findings provide valuable guidance for deploying optimized detection systems on resource-constrained edge devices for agricultural applications. © 2025 ASABE. All rights reserved.
