AD  - University of South Dakota, USD AI Research, Department of Computer Science, United States
AD  - University of South Dakota, Biomedical & Translational Sciences, Sanford School of Medicine, United States
AB  - Transformer-based UNet variants have recently achieved impressive performance in medical image segmentation; however, their computational cost remains prohibitive for real-time and resource-constrained applications. To address this limitation, we propose DynaFormer, a Dynamic Dual-Attention Transformer architecture that jointly models spatial and channel interactions while maintaining linear-complexity cross-scale fusion. Specifically, DynaFormer integrates (i) spatially adaptive pixel attention to prune redundant spatial dependencies and (ii) channel-wise Flash Attention to enable efficient global semantic coupling, together with a lightweight convolutional bottleneck inception module for local structural encoding. Furthermore, we introduce a gated cross-scale fusion decoder that performs multi-level feature interaction with linear complexity in spatial tokens. Experiments on two publicly available benchmark datasets, including the Synapse multi-organ CT dataset and the ISIC-2018 skin lesion segmentation dataset, demonstrate strong performance across different anatomical structures and imaging modalities. In particular, DynaFormer achieves the best average Dice score of 81.96% with the competitive HD95 of 17.30 on Synapse, and an average Dice score of 90.47% on ISIC-2018, while maintaining comparable parameter complexity and reducing FLOPs by up to 32% compared to Swin-UNet, MISS-Former, and HiFormer. These results indicate that DynaFormer provides an effective accuracy-efficiency trade-off and represents an effective and efficient architecture for real-time clinical deployment. The code is publicly available at https://github.com/pis2016004-prog/DynaFormer. © 2026 IEEE.
