AD  - Sudamericano Institute of Technology, Department of Electricity, Cuenca, Ecuador
AB  - The study presents a comparative evaluation of six computer vision architectures applied to the automatic detection of maize foliar conditions, including Healthy Leaf, Northern Leaf Blight, Common Rust, and Gray Leaf Spot. For this purpose, the models Faster R-CNN, SSD, YOLOv8m, YOLOv8n, MobileNetV3-Large, and EfficientNetV2-RW were trained and analyzed using a dataset collected in the field and manually annotated. The methodology included data augmentation techniques, standardized training for 20 epochs, and evaluation using widely used object detection metrics such as mAP@0.5, mAP@0.5:0.95, average recall, performance by object size, and inference speed. The results show that YOLOv8-based architectures achieve the best overall performance, with YOLOv8m reaching an mAP@0.5 of 0.72 and an mAP@0.5:0.95 of 0.41, while YOLOv8n achieves 32 FPS with a reduced model size of 8.7 MB, making it suitable for embedded implementations on drones. In contrast, Faster R-CNN and SSD present limitations in detecting small lesions, while lightweight models such as MobileNetV3-Large and EfficientNetV2-RW offer lower precision but high speed. These findings confirm the potential of the YOLOv8 family for real agricultural applications aimed at early monitoring of diseases and foliar damage in maize crops.  © 2026 IEEE.
