TI  - Eyes in the Thicket: A Neural Gaze for Invisible Wildlife Encounters
PY  - 2025
T2  - 2025 Control Instrumentation System Conference, CISCON 2025
DO  - 10.1109/CISCON66933.2025.11337723
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-105033495486&doi=10.1109%2fCISCON66933.2025.11337723&partnerID=40&md5=981bbc4a34e84d412dd54275a273fe0c
AD  - Panimalar Engineering College, Computer Science and Engineering, Chennai, 600123, India
AD  - Botho University, Faculty of Engineering and Technology, Botswana
AB  - ResNet50-driven wild animal detection systems represent a significant leap toward harmonizing ecological surveillance with intelligent computation. This paper introduces a real-time monitoring solution that leverages convolutional deep learning and embedded vision to identify elusive wildlife in complex environments. As human expansion interfaces more frequently with natural habitats, the urgency to detect and prevent wild animal incursions has escalated. Traditional systems falter under dynamic conditions - low light, camouflage, partial visibility - wherein animals blend seamlessly with the background. Our system addresses this through residual learning blocks capable of preserving low-level spatial cues critical for animal detection amidst occlusions and background clutter. Powered by a live feed pipeline and optimized using transfer learning, the model adapts to diverse species with minimal retraining effort. The GSM-integrated alert system transmits notifications instantaneously upon detection, offering a non-intrusive, proactive response layer. Deployment scenarios range from farm borders to remote forest outposts. Comparative experiments benchmark ResNet50 against YOLOv3, SSD, and Fast R-CNN, demonstrating superior accuracy and latency balance. This system further integrates energy-conscious hardware (Raspberry Pi and thermal cameras) to ensure long-term, low-power operation in rural terrains. Our research contributes not just a technological framework but a shift toward ethical, responsive coexistence between humans and wildlife. The proposed system acts as both observer and guardian - an unblinking neural gaze embedded within the thicket. © 2025 IEEE.
