AB  - In this paper, we are going to introduce a hybrid CNN-Transformer model to achieve high-precision image classification and overcome the disadvantages of both convolutional and attention-only models. Traditional CNNs work well at modeling local spatial patterns based on inductive bias and weight sharing, but and fail to model long-range dependencies, whereas Transformers are able to model global context very well but require large datasets and high computational resources. Architecture The architecture is based on a single workflow, which includes convolutional feature learning, adaptive tokenization, positional embedding, and global context aggregate such that multi-scale representation learning becomes possible. A thorough methodology is embraced, with the proper selection of the dataset, standard image preprocessing, and the massive data augmentation to promote generalization. Hyperparameters are optimized in a systematic way to have the accuracy, stability of convergence, and computer efficiency. Through experimental analysis, it is shown that the hybrid model outperforms the standalone models of CNN and Transformer in the classification accuracy, feature discriminability, and resistance to visual variations. The findings show enhanced contextual reasoning at the expense of spatial accuracy, which has rendered the architecture applicable in intricate visual recognition activities. Although the study is effective, it also characterizes major challenges associated with the computational overhead, memory use and scalability, especially to edge and embedded applications. © 2026 IEEE.
