AD  - Graduate School of Science And Technology, Nihon University, Precision Machinery Engineering, Chiba, Japan
AD  - Nihon University, Dept. of Precision Machinery Engineering, College of Science And Technology, Chiba, Japan
AB  - In recent years, research on image segmentation using deep learning for medical applications has flourished. While the UNet architecture achieves high accuracy in medical image segmentation, it struggles to capture long-range features. To overcome this limitation, TransUNet combines the UNet architecture with a Transformer. However, TransUNet has a large number of parameters, resulting in high computational load and memory consumption, which makes deployment on resource-constrained edge devices difficult. To address this issue, we propose a structure more suitable for edge implementation by incorporating mechanisms that reduce computational resources within the TransUNet architecture. Specifically, we introduce a dynamic window mechanism, where the window size adaptively changes according to attention scores. This reduces computational load and memory usage, enabling more efficient processing. Furthermore, by making the windows independent, the structure is optimized for parallel processing. We compare the performance of the proposed dynamic window TransUNet with the existing original architecture on the Synapse multi-organ segmentation dataset. Results show that our approach achieves comparable accuracy to the original TransUNet while significantly reducing memory consumption and computational cost, making it more practical for deployment on resource-constrained edge devices. © 2025 IEEE.
