AD  - East West University, Dept. of Cse, Aftabnagar, Dhaka, Bangladesh
AB  - Mango is a profitable fruit crop whose production is threatened by demanding foliar diseases whose diagnoses through slow and subjective manual inspection are painstaking. While CNN-based deep learning methods have been promising, they require immense annotated data and suffer from poor generalization in diverse field conditions. In this work, an SSL-ViT model for robust mango leaf disease classification under sparse labeled data is developed. The model was pre-trained using a SimCLR-like augmentation pipeline and fine-tuned over labeled datasets, with the evaluation on several splits, including an aggregation dataset for generalization testing. Interpretability was achieved using Grad-CAM visualizations, which confirmed that predictions aligned with disease symptoms. The framework achieved over 97% accuracy on all datasets, with reduced class variance in the aggregation set, and always concentrated in biologically important regions. These results highlight the potential of transformer-based self-supervised learning to provide precise, understandable, and implementable systems for crop disease diagnosis. Future work will experiment with other SSL methods, field-gathered data, and partnerships with agriculture organizations.  © 2026 IEEE.
