AD  - Hassan 1st University, Mdset Laboratory, Faculty Of Sciences And Techniques, Settat, Morocco
AB  - Hybrid AI and IoT monitoring is increasingly expected to support not only recognizing symptoms but also planning early warnings and interventions in field conditions. To gather current evidence, we perform a PRISMA-guided systematic literature review of 30 studies published between 2020 and 2026, focusing on the "sensing, diagnosis, forecasting, action"pipeline in crop protection. We structure the review around three research questions on data types, model families, and deployment challenges, based on various synthesis works that connect computer vision, sensing, and data fusion. The reviewed studies confirm that RGB imaging is the main input for diagnosis, while microclimate IoT data are increasingly used to monitor environmental factors that affect disease risk. Physiological sensing, such as VOC and ultrasound, shows promise for detecting issues before symptoms show up in specialized experiments. Methodologically, real-time one-stage detectors are notable in detection studies relevant to field conditions, including cases from Morocco. These detectors are supported by hybrid architectures that manage complex backgrounds and sequential signals. Dataset imbalance, a lack of robustness when transitioning from lab to real-world use, and the limits of edge resources still hinder progress. This highlights the need for both efficiency and clarity in deploying reliable systems. Overall, the evidence suggests a move toward dependable end-to-end systems. These systems will combine multimodal sensing, efficient edge processing, and time-based modeling to improve crop protection. © 2026 IEEE.
